From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wize-logic <wizelogic@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:39:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] recomp: --reg-locals keeps a function's integer registers in C locals

The generated code reads and writes the guest's integer registers as the
globals g_eax .. g_edi and g_esp (`eax` is a macro for g_eax). Guest
memory is reached through volatile, may_alias accesses, so the compiler
has to assume that any guest store may change any of those globals: after
each store it reloads every register it needs, and every register write
is a store to memory. In Halo's hot functions that is a load or store of
a global around nearly every guest instruction, and the EA of every
esp-relative access is computed again because esp was reloaded.

--reg-locals extends x87_locals.py to the registers: in each function
they are locals (_r_eax ..), loaded where they are first needed after
memory was the truth, and written back (those that may have been written)
before every statement that calls out or leaves, exactly as the x87 state
is. A register counts as written when it is assigned, incremented or
decremented, or passed to a macro that may assign it (SET_LO8, SET_HI8,
SET_LO16, PUSH32's stack pointer, POP32's both, and any all-capitals macro
not known to only read its arguments). A block over several lines (a rep
string loop) is rewritten line by line. Statements that call out run on
the globals, unchanged, between the write-back and the next load; a
statement whose register has its address taken runs on memory. With
--reg-locals the pass covers every function, not only those that use the
x87; without --x87-locals the x87 macros stay as they are.

The globals are not current at RECOMP_BACKEDGE or at a guest access the
runtime emulates. A runtime that switches guest threads or runs interrupt
work there must save and restore the register file around it (each guest
thread has its own), and run interrupt work on a stack of its own rather
than below a stale g_esp.

The header's SIMD helpers (XMM_*, MMX_*) are taken as not leaving the
function, for both options: they touch guest memory and the SIMD
registers only.
